## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `Thread.ResetAbort()` and catching `ThreadAbortException` are also dead code paths.

---

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on cancellation | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` or `Join(timeout)` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic as the old ThreadAbortException handler
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; loop condition will exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful exit
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't exit — it's stuck in uncooperative code.
            // Log a warning; you cannot force-kill a managed thread in .NET 8.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to Supporting Methods

```csharp
// BEFORE: may block indefinitely with no way to interrupt
private WorkItem GetNextItem() { /* blocking call */ }

// AFTER: accepts token so it can be interrupted
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // If using a BlockingCollection<T>:
    return _queue.Take(token);

    // If using a manual wait:
    // token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout);
    // token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
}
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| `GetNextItem()` blocks forever, ignoring cancellation | Pass `CancellationToken` to all blocking calls — cancellation only works if the code **cooperates** |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Implement `IDisposable`; leaking CTS leaks timers/callbacks |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions |
| Assuming `Cancel()` stops the thread instantly | It only takes effect at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
| No fallback if thread won't exit | There is **no equivalent** to force-kill in .NET 8. If the code truly can't cooperate, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` |

---

### The Fundamental Shift

**.NET Framework:** The runtime forcibly injects `ThreadAbortException` at any point — preemptive.  
**.NET 8:** The code must **voluntarily** check `CancellationToken` — cooperative. If your `ProcessItem` does long CPU work without checking the token, cancellation is delayed until the next loop iteration.
